00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
when you think of the union of soviet  socialist republics you probably don't  think of millionaires the soviet union  was  after all a communist country built upon  the ideals of economic equality  that said human beings like to do their  own thing and getting rich in a country  that you're not supposed to is hardly  unimaginable  so did it ever happen were there any  millionaires in the ussr and if so how  did the people there react  so there are some difficulties in  answering this question since there were  limitations on how money in the soviet  union worked  for example quantities of cars toilet  paper and foods were strictly controlled  and so getting more of these was reliant  on political connections not cash  and also it's not like you could be so  rich that you could retire early since  for most lengthy unemployment was  against the law  that said if you had the political  connections and the right job you could  live a pretty comfortable life  comparable to millionaires in the west  for example many high-ranking ministers  who served under stalin had very little  concept of money  since as government ministers they could  ask for something and simply be brought  it with no money leaving their pockets  one funny anecdote which demonstrates  this is from when stalin and his  ministers were going for a walk around  moscow  and doing so they saw an old lady  struggling to cross the road  stalin helped her to cross and then  wanted to give her some money to help  her out  one problem though neither stalin nor  his ministers carried any money with  them since they never used it and the  old lady got squat  anyway to answer the question as to  whether or not there were any people who  had the equivalent of a million dollars  openly in the ussr the answer was yes  although it didn't happen until very  late in its life after gorbachev had  reformed the economy to allow some  private enterprise some people started  to make a lot of money  particularly from importing luxury goods  from the west and one man who did this  was artium tarazov who in 1989 declared  on television that he was the first  person in the ussr to be worth millions  of rubles  which wasn't millions in terms of us  dollars but given that for most soviet  citizens their yearly wage was between  1500 and 3  000 rubles it was a lot so how did the  soviet people react  well very well in the all congratulated  tarazovon just kidding they were  outraged  protests sprang up in major cities  politicians denounced the acquisition of  capital on that scale and many called  for tarazov to be imprisoned  his accounts were frozen and the kgb  launched an investigation into him but  ultimately they found nothing and so he  was allowed to go back to his business  and soon after he became a millionaire  in the traditional sense  now not a lot is known about tarazov in  his business but what is certain is that  his relations with the soviet government  were pretty awful and it wasn't long  until he fled the country and eventually  settled in the uk  despite all of this hubbub though more  and more soviet citizens became  wealthier and wealthier over the next  two years  and when the ussr collapsed and many  public assets were sold off it was these  people mostly who brought shares in them  and whose wealth then subsequently  exploded  giving some of the post-soviet states  the oligarchs that they're known for  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bisonette kelly money maker marvin  cassell moe rob waterhouse james  castaneda jerry lamdin gustav swan aaron  the whites jordan longley marcus asner  charles the first rasheed ali coppertone  maggie paxkowski lexi schwinn miss is it  matthew shipley robert wetzel spinning  three plates spencer lightfoot  corso wolfe anthony beckett and winston  caywood
 

